<article class="post">
<header class="post-header wrap-narrow">
  <span class="eyebrow">Release 2.3.0 · Correctness</span>
  <h1><%= post["title"] %></h1>
  <p class="post-meta"><time datetime="<%= post["date"] %>"><%= post["display_date"] %></time> · <%= post["read_minutes"] %> min read</p>
  <p class="post-lede">2.3.0 is mostly about answers that were wrong without saying so. A dropped database whose rows a query could still return. An <code>UPDATE</code> of an edge&rsquo;s <code>_from</code> that changed every field but that one. A metrics token that the documented header could not deliver. And a two-phase-commit API that had never written a document. Each is fixed, or in the last case removed, and there are two additions: query latency on <code>/metrics</code>, and reading a document inside a transaction.</p>
  <p class="post-note">Every response on this page was captured from a 2.3.0 release build on a throwaway server. The behaviour before 2.3.0 is described from the code and the regression tests that reproduce it, not from a captured session.</p>
</header>

<div class="post-body wrap-narrow">
<nav class="post-toc" aria-label="In this post">
  <p class="post-toc-title">In this post</p>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#cache">A cached answer that outlived its database</a></li>
    <li><a href="#indexes">Indexes changed the answer, the cache did not</a></li>
    <li><a href="#edge">An edge that would not move</a></li>
    <li><a href="#metrics">Query latency on <code>/metrics</code>, and a token that never got through</a></li>
    <li><a href="#distributed">A two-phase commit that never wrote anything</a></li>
